{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-347888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(x-1),\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=0$ to approximate $y(1).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(1)≈$  \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-347888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(0)=2.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=y(x-1),$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=2(0-1)=-2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(0)$ and ${y}^{′}(0)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0) \\\\  & =2+(-2)(x-0) \\\\  & =2-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(1)$ using $L(1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ≈L(1) \\\\  & =2-2(1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-347888" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccdf2665e3afb1bacb9f9fc7d2163dfc8c3bf6bfd4d4535f4ccc0d0e48d110db"} {"question" "q-347888" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-347888" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-50119" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(x-4),\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=0$ to approximate $y(1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-50119" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(0)=1.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=y(x-4),$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=1(0-4)=-4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(0)$ and ${y}^{′}(0)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0) \\\\  & =1+(-4)(x-0) \\\\  & =1-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(1)$ using $L(1).$ We get\n\n$$\ny(1)≈L(1)=1-4(1)=-3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-50119" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccdf2665e3afb1bacb9f9fc7d2163dfc8c3bf6bfd4d4535f4ccc0d0e48d110db"} {"question" "q-50119" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-50119" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-50119" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-353288" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{y},\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=1$ to approximate $y(\\frac{3}{2}).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$  \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-353288" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(1)=2.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{y},$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(1)$ and ${y}^{′}(1)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1) \\\\  & =2+(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ using $L(\\frac{3}{2}).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{3}{2}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-353288" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccdf2665e3afb1bacb9f9fc7d2163dfc8c3bf6bfd4d4535f4ccc0d0e48d110db"} {"question" "q-353288" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-353288" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-353287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}},\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ to approximate $y(\\frac{3}{4}).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(\\frac{3}{4})≈$  \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-353287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=\\frac{1}{2}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})(x-\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{4}.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{y}},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and ${y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(\\frac{1}{2})+{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+(-1)(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(\\frac{3}{4})$ using $L(\\frac{3}{4}).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{3}{4}) & ≈L(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-353287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccdf2665e3afb1bacb9f9fc7d2163dfc8c3bf6bfd4d4535f4ccc0d0e48d110db"} {"question" "q-353287" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-353287" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-y,\\,y(2)=0.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=2$ to approximate $y(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(2)=0.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}-y,$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(2)={2}^{2}-0=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)(x-2) \\\\  & =0+4(x-2) \\\\  & =4(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(3)$ using $L(3).$ We get\n\n$$\ny(3)≈L(3)=4(3-2)=4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccdf2665e3afb1bacb9f9fc7d2163dfc8c3bf6bfd4d4535f4ccc0d0e48d110db"} {"question" "q-116850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-347893" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-2y,\\,y(2)=3\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Fill in the empty box in the column for $Δy$ in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-347893" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, because the initial condition is $y(2)=3,$ we fill $y=3$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-2y \\\\  & =4(2)-2(3) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $2$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $2$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-347893" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccdf2665e3afb1bacb9f9fc7d2163dfc8c3bf6bfd4d4535f4ccc0d0e48d110db"} {"question" "q-347893" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-347893" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-353301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\sqrt{x+{y}^{2}-3},\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Fill in the empty box in the column for $Δy$ in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-353301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, because the initial condition is $y(0)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{0+{2}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $1$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-353301" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccdf2665e3afb1bacb9f9fc7d2163dfc8c3bf6bfd4d4535f4ccc0d0e48d110db"} {"question" "q-353301" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-353301" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-353296" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+y,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{5}.$ Fill in the empty box in the column for $Δy$ in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-353296" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+y \\\\  & =2(1)+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $\\frac{4}{5}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-353296" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccdf2665e3afb1bacb9f9fc7d2163dfc8c3bf6bfd4d4535f4ccc0d0e48d110db"} {"question" "q-353296" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-353296" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{5}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy+1,\\,y(1)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=-1,$ we fill $y=-1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy+1 \\\\  & =1(-1)+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccdf2665e3afb1bacb9f9fc7d2163dfc8c3bf6bfd4d4535f4ccc0d0e48d110db"} {"question" "q-116876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-86973" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-x+5y,\\,y(-3)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=3?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-86973" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, because the initial condition is $y(-3)=1,$ we fill $y=1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-x+5y \\\\  & =-(-3)+5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $8$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =8\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $8$ | $24$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-86973" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccdf2665e3afb1bacb9f9fc7d2163dfc8c3bf6bfd4d4535f4ccc0d0e48d110db"} {"question" "q-86973" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-86973" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "24"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-86973" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "24"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3xy,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3xy \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+6 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $8$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccdf2665e3afb1bacb9f9fc7d2163dfc8c3bf6bfd4d4535f4ccc0d0e48d110db"} {"question" "q-116777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "14"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-2y,\\,y(2)=4\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x-2y \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $-2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =4+(-2) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |\n| $3$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccdf2665e3afb1bacb9f9fc7d2163dfc8c3bf6bfd4d4535f4ccc0d0e48d110db"} {"question" "q-116780" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116780" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116780" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,y(2)={e}^{2}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(\\frac{5}{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5}{2}-2=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | ${e}^{2}$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | ${e}^{2}$ | $4$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | ${e}^{2}$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | ${e}^{2}$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | ${e}^{2}+2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(\\frac{5}{2})≈{e}^{2}+2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccdf2665e3afb1bacb9f9fc7d2163dfc8c3bf6bfd4d4535f4ccc0d0e48d110db"} {"question" "q-116800" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116800" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{e}^{2}+1"] ["math" "{e}^{2}+2"] ["math" "{e}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "{e}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "{e}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116800" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{2}+2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-353306" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2y}{x+1},\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Fill in the empty box in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-1}} | $$ | $$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-353306" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, because the initial condition is $y(0)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{2y}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2)}{0+1} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $4$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(\\frac{1}{2})≈4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-353306" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccdf2665e3afb1bacb9f9fc7d2163dfc8c3bf6bfd4d4535f4ccc0d0e48d110db"} {"question" "q-353306" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-353306" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116798" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(x-5)+4,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{3}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\,?\\,$ | $$ | $$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116798" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, because the initial condition is $y(0)=-1,$ we fill $y=-1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-5)+4 \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (0-5)+4 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $9$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $9$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-1+3 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $9$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(\\frac{1}{3})≈2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116798" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ccdf2665e3afb1bacb9f9fc7d2163dfc8c3bf6bfd4d4535f4ccc0d0e48d110db"} {"question" "q-116798" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116798" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "q-116798" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/state.json" "file_sha256" "e61fa082b26e9eeaaabc5bab69ee7e7eb569f4f03b64a2aa8580c48af33d0768"} {"question" "e-536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = 2xy, \\qquad y(1)=2,\n$$\n\nuse the tangent line approximation at $x=1$ to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/example-536.json" "file_sha256" "c71e8615f7e325af6d4423efba38c2d11b31dd0bd49e948098e4e842f028c163"} {"question" "e-536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(1)+{y}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(1) = 2.$ We also know that $y'(x) = 2xy,$ so\n\n$$\ny'(1) = 2(1)(2) = 4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(1)$ and $y'(1)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+(4)(x-1) \\\\  & =2+4x-4 \\\\  & =4x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right)$ using $L\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right).$ We get\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac 3 2 \\right)\\approx L\\left(\\dfrac 3 2 \\right) = 4\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right) - 2 = 4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/example-536.json" "file_sha256" "c71e8615f7e325af6d4423efba38c2d11b31dd0bd49e948098e4e842f028c163"} {"question" "e-7700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' =7x-2y, \\qquad y(1)=-4\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $\\Delta y$ in the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1}{10}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | $\\, ? \\,$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/example-7700.json" "file_sha256" "cbdd9515c4c9196e9e543d441ec37be55c555971d72cd8773a7e81aacf17c68e"} {"question" "e-7700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=-4,$ we fill $y=-4$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =7x-2y \\\\  & =7\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ | $15$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =15\\cdot \\frac{1}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-4$ | $15$ | $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/example-7700.json" "file_sha256" "cbdd9515c4c9196e9e543d441ec37be55c555971d72cd8773a7e81aacf17c68e"} {"question" "e-9340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' =2y^2-x^2, \\qquad y(1)=3\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/example-9340.json" "file_sha256" "1dde178a5287557916c8f15138b8b1606d0bd13852dd6e9120e578bb270e4be3"} {"question" "e-9340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2-1 = 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =18-1 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =17\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ | $17$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =3+17 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ | $17$ |\n| $2$ | $20$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx 20.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037231/example-9340.json" "file_sha256" "1dde178a5287557916c8f15138b8b1606d0bd13852dd6e9120e578bb270e4be3"}] "usage" {"e-7700" [] "e-9340" [] "q-50119" [] "e-536" []} "basis" 1264 "retractions" [[17592186078964 :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$y^{′} = y(x - 4), y(0) = 1$.\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x = 0$ to approximate $y(1)$."] [17592186078245 :question/problem "For the initial value problem\n\n$y' = 2xy, \\qquad y(1)=2$,\n\nuse the tangent line approximation at $x=1$ to approximate $y\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right)$."] [17592186078245 :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= y(a) + y^{′}(a)(x - a) \\\\\n&= y(1) + y^{′}(1)(x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(1) = 2$. We also know that\n\n$$\ny'(x) = 2xy\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\ny'(1) = 2(1)(2) = 4\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(1)$ and $y'(1)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= 2 + (4)(x - 1) \\\\\n&= 2 + 4x - 4 \\\\\n&= 4x - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right)\n$$\n\nusing\n\n$$\nL\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right)\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac 3 2 \\right)\\approx L\\left(\\dfrac 3 2 \\right) = 4\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right) - 2 = 4\n$$"] [17592186078287 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y' =7x-2y, \\qquad y(1)=-4$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $\\Delta y$ in the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1}{10}$?\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ |  |  | $\\,? \\,$ |"] [17592186078287 :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=-4$, we fill $y=-4$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 7x - 2y \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 1 - 2 \\cdot (-4) \\\\\n&= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-4$ | $15$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot \\frac{1}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $-4$ | $15$ | $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ |"] [17592186078298 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y' =2y^2-x^2, \\qquad y(1)=3$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(2)$."] [17592186078298 :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2$, we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2-1 = 1\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= 2y^{2} - x^{2} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 3^{2} - 1^{2} \\\\\n&= 18 - 1 \\\\\n&= 17\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= 17 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 17\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ | $17$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{new} &= x + Δx \\\\\n&= 1 + 1 \\\\\n&= 2 \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\n&= 3 + 17 \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $17$ | $17$ |\n| $2$ | $20$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(2) \\approx 20\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186076996 17592186076997 17592186076998 17592186076999 17592186077000] "immutable_fields" [[17592186076995 :answer-field/correct] [17592186076995 :answer-field/id] [17592186076995 :answer-field/key] [17592186076995 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
